Web text input: Flutter's per-field configuration rules, password element, keyboard layout and IME placement
in gpui_web.

What (gpui_web only):
- `text_editing.rs` (new, pure): the IME mirror's attributes for a `TextInputConfiguration`, ported from Flutter's web
  engine (text_editing/input_type.dart, input_action.dart, text_capitalization.dart, autofill_hint.dart,
  text_editing.dart's `applyConfiguration`): `autocomplete` is the autofill hint's token or "off" (upstream tied it
  to `suggestions`), `enterkeyhint` only on iOS and Android (upstream set it everywhere), `autocorrect`,
  `autocapitalize`, `spellcheck`. `input_mode`: each `KeyboardType`'s `inputmode`, written whenever the virtual
  keyboard is enabled (upstream always wrote "text", ignoring patch 0005's keyboard type). `infer_edit`: the
  prefix/suffix diff `register_input` ran inline, moved here unchanged so Flutter's delta cases test it.
- Secure fields: `element_kind` is Flutter's `createDomElement` plus `obscureText` (`applyConfiguration`): the
  mirror is an `<input type=password>` while the focused field is `secure`, a `<textarea>` otherwise (GPUI has no
  multiline flag, and an `<input>` strips newlines). `ImeMirror::apply_configuration` swaps the element in place
  when the kind changes, carrying over placement, `inputmode`, `readonly` and focus; the IME listeners sit on a
  `display: contents` container, so they outlive the swap. On touch an `<input>`'s Enter is a keydown only (no
  `insertLineBreak`), so it is dispatched as a key, as Flutter's `maybeSendAction` does.
- `WebWindow::update_ime_position` (was a no-op): moves the mirror onto the caret, as Flutter's `placeElement`, so
  a desktop IME's candidate window opens at the caret; never mid-composition (Flutter's `updateElementPlacement`)
  and never on touch (iOS would pan the page to a focused field near the keyboard).
- Table tests from Flutter's text_editing_test.dart: 14 configuration rows, 5 element rows, 7 input modes, 8 edit
  rows (7 of Flutter's `TextEditingDeltaState` cases and one for the caret-anchored diff). They run on the host through
  scripts/patch-test.sh's `web-pure` crate.

Why: GPUI apps on the web got the wrong keyboard for number, email and URL fields, no autofill tokens, an action key
on desktop, IME candidate windows in the page's top-left corner, and password fields keyboards could learn. Flutter's
web engine has fixed each of these; gpui_web keeps one mirror element where Flutter keeps one per field, so the rules
port, the elements don't. Not ported: Flutter's `type=text` for a secure `<input>` whose autofill hint lacks
"password" (`AutofillInfo.applyToDomElement`), Flutter's per-browser blur and refocus rules (GPUI focus, not the DOM,
owns the connection), autofill forms, the delta model.

